average high shows no clear change: \u22120.11\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.24 to +0.03) that includes zero. July and August highs have edged down across the full record; the 1910s summers were the hottest in it. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.53\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.23 to +0.83) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.55\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.18 to +0.91). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 11 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; 7 months fell.","t_rain":"Beckley has gotten drier","p_rain":"Beckley's yearly precipitation has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.49 inches per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.85 to \u22120.14), about \u22125.8 inches since 1907. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 43.5 inches a year. The wettest year was 1935 (57.41 in); the driest, 1965 (32.62 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 7 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}